CREDITS
=======

FrameFusion derives from VideoSlicer, by the same author: the preview, the
timeline and the FFmpeg handling come from it. The interface theme, the
window shell and the logo style come from AndrOsint and TagFaces, also by the
same author.

The logo and the icon are set in Panchang, a typeface by Indian Type Foundry
(ITF), which owns its trademarks and copyright
<https://www.fontshare.com/fonts/panchang>. They contain outlines and
images only: the font is not distributed with this program.


THIRD-PARTY SOFTWARE
====================

FrameFusion does not include FFmpeg. When the user asks for it, the program
downloads the official Windows build ("release essentials", by Gyan Doshi,
<https://www.gyan.dev/ffmpeg/builds/>, the build linked from
<https://ffmpeg.org/download.html>) into the tools/ffmpeg/ folder next to
it, keeping ffmpeg.exe together with its LICENSE and README files. FFmpeg
<https://ffmpeg.org> is a separate program under the GNU LGPL v2.1 or later
(GPL v2 or later when built with GPL components, as that build is); it is
not part of this program and is run as an external executable. FFmpeg's own
license and source code are available at <https://ffmpeg.org/legal.html>.
An ffmpeg already present in the PATH, or the one shipped by the optional
Python package imageio-ffmpeg (BSD-2-Clause), is used in the same way.

FrameFusion uses the Python libraries CustomTkinter (MIT, with its
dependencies darkdetect, BSD-3-Clause, and packaging, Apache 2.0 or
BSD-2-Clause), Pillow (MIT-CMU), NumPy (BSD-3-Clause) and OpenCV
(opencv-python-headless, Apache License 2.0; that package ships an FFmpeg
library, opencv_videoio_ffmpeg*.dll, under the GNU LGPL v2.1, as documented
in its LICENSE-3RD-PARTY.txt), and fpdf2 (GNU LGPL v3, with its dependencies
fonttools, MIT, and defusedxml, PSF License), which writes the PDF report.
Each of them remains under its own license, as does the Python interpreter
(PSF License). The PDF report embeds subsets of fonts installed on the
user's computer (Segoe UI and Consolas on Windows, DejaVu or Liberation
elsewhere), as their licenses allow; no font is distributed with the program. The executable built with
PyInstaller contains them; the release archive includes their license files
in the licenses folder.
